messages.content:
{
 "location": "Rialto booth",
 "visible": "There is one original interview reel, one usable duplicate, a grease pencil and a clean paper sleeve. Alma consented to a family delivery. Rita controls the master.",
 "private_facts_available_for_disclosure": {
  "song": "The Rivoli used the same song to bring up its house lights.",
  "ring": "Rita's mother tapped the chipped ring twice on the projection booth glass.",
  "application": "Rita has an unfinished application for a producer's job in Pittsburgh."
 },
 "present_owner": "Rita",
 "heard": "Phil puts an addressed envelope on the table and takes one pace back. 'Yours to decide, Rita. I should've asked first.'",
 "your_memory": "On this shift you told Phil the story of the ring.",
 "previous_beat": {
  "your_action": "seal_copy",
  "your_words": "Duplicate's sealed. Original's staying. You tell her the family copy's the one she can keep, and the master's mine to log. Don't put the ring in the conversation with her. She doesn't need a second story tonight. She needs the reel and the name on the sleeve and to be able to close the door after she watches it. That's the whole job.",
  "spoken_disclosure": null
 },
 "possible_actions": {
  "give_master": "Put the original reel into Phil's hands for delivery.",
  "keep_master": "Keep the original at Rita's console.",
  "seal_copy": "Seal a family copy for Alma's daughter and keep the original with Rita.",
  "leave_alone": "Take the original back to the booth and leave Phil here."
 },
 "optional_disclosure_keys": [
  "song",
  "ring",
  "application"
 ]
}

{
  "action": "keep_master",
  "detail": "I put my thumb on the spine of the canister in the third slot. The grease is still warm on it from where I logged it at four-fifteen. I don't lift it out. I don't slide it toward the edge of the table where his hand would find it. My thumb just sits there, the flat pad pressed into the ridge of the metal, and I can feel the little nick where the latch catches. Phil is a pace back. The envelope is face-up on the table, his handwriting on the front, ALMA K. in block capitals, and the grease pencil is lying between us like a small grey bone. I pick up the pencil. I don't write anything. I put it back down, two inches closer to me. The duplicate is already sealed, already in the clean sleeve, already his to carry to the car if he wants to. That's done. The original is in the rack and my thumb is on it and I am not going to say the word *no* because the word *no* is a door and I don't want him to hear me close one. I just let my thumb stay. I turn back to the monitor. The room tone is running. The icebox hum is in there, the second set of wheels, the floor manager's quarter. I put the volume up one click. His footsteps don't come closer. They don't come back, either. I hear the envelope shift a little, the paper catching the air, and then the booth door clicks shut and the hum gets bigger and I am alone with the reel and my thumb and the four seconds the minute hand is wrong.",
  "intention": "The master stays. The duplicate was already sealed and is his to deliver. I am not making this a speech. I am not making it a refusal. My thumb is on the canister and that is the whole sentence. The envelope is his. The pencil is back where I put it. The door is shut. I am not going to add a second story to the ring. I am not going to hand him the name of the song. I am not going to fold the application out from behind the lining and let him see the four evenings in the first line. The reel is in the rack. My thumb is on it. The hum is in the headphones. That is enough. That is the whole job.",
  "words": ""
}
